body:
The README says the repository is public for inspection but remains proprietary and all rights are reserved. GitHub also reports no license or contribution guide.

You mentioned that Cribble is open source and contributions are welcome. Could we make that repository-visible by adding the intended open-source license and a short contribution policy?

That would clarify:

- what contributors may use, modify, and redistribute;
- whether external pull requests are welcome;
- any issue/design step expected before larger changes;
- test/build requirements;
- AI-assistance disclosure expectations, if any.

I have a fork and would like to contribute small, tested fixes upstream. I do not want to assume terms that conflict with the current README.

## Problem

Public bug reports and browser evidence cannot currently identify which exact Cribble revision produced the observed behavior. That makes production-to-source comparisons ambiguous, especially when the public deployment and repository main may move independently.

Cribble already owns its Vercel deployment configuration, and Vercel provides the exact deployment commit through `VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA`.

## Proposed contract

Expose a privacy-safe deployment revision on `/api/status` as:

```text
X-Cribble-Revision: <40 lowercase hexadecimal characters>
```

Implementation requirements:

- read only `VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA`;
- accept only an exact 40-character hexadecimal commit SHA;
- normalize valid values to lowercase;
- omit the header when the variable is missing or malformed;
- add the header to both successful and fallback status responses;
- attach it outside the 60-second cached status payload so deployment identity is not cached as service data;
- do not expose branch/ref names, paths, arbitrary environment variables, tokens, or internal deployment metadata;
- preserve the existing response body and cache-control contract.

## Acceptance criteria

- Valid sentinel SHA is returned exactly in a production-server probe.
- Missing, short, non-hexadecimal, branch-shaped, path-shaped, and secret-shaped values produce no revision header.
- Success and fallback status responses use the same revision behavior.
- Existing status body, `s-maxage`, and stale-while-revalidate semantics remain unchanged.
- Build and browser evidence can record the revision without requiring privileged deployment access.
